ralph/progress.txt:191:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, backup-restore-rehearsal instance (BE-0399 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_backup_restore_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/backup/backup_restore_rehearsal_test.go's new canonical pair TestBackupRestoreRehearsalCoversCallSites + TestBackupRestoreRehearsalPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Backup Restore Rehearsal Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: backup and restore rehearsal tests (BE-0399)" subsection; TWENTY-FIRST worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0398 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestBackupRestoreRehearsal ./...`, CI step name `Backup restore rehearsal tests`, verify.sh prefix #24 inserted between BE-0398's #23 and the trailing optionals renumbered 24->25/25->26/26->27/27->28 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0398's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 18 substrings: internal/controlplane/backup, backup_restore_rehearsal_test.go, deterministic, FileReporter, NewFileReporter, ErrNoBackupRecorded, Status.Fresh, MaxAge, RFC3339, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, rehearsal, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0399-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a single-file leaf-package state-reporting chokepoint with a documented closed set of error sentinels and a tiny external-input parser (BE-0400 external Dokploy smoke, BE-0401 release build, BE-0402 config validation): (1) `SCENARIO-TABLE-DRIVEN MULTI-SHAPE OBSERVATION VERIFIER — ONE assess() FUNCTION FEEDS BOTH MEMBERS, RETURNS REASON STRINGS NOT t.Errorf CALLS` — the canonical pair shares an `assessRehearsalObservation(scen, status, err) []string` helper that takes a scenario and an (output, error) tuple and returns a list of human-readable failure reasons. The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests and calls t.Errorf per reason inline; the burst member's worker goroutines call the same helper but record (worker, iteration, scenario, reasons) into a mutex-guarded slice that the main goroutine drains and reports after wg.Wait() — no testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged), same shape as BE-0395/BE-0396/BE-0397's burst patterns. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set table feeds both members AND the chokepoint surfaces a tuple (status, err) instead of a single verdict: a single string-returning assess() function is the source-of-truth predicate; iterate it in subtests for coverage, batch-collect-and-report for burst. (2) `CLOSED-SET ERROR-SHAPE ENUMERATION VIA TYPED KIND CONSTANT (Nil / Sentinel / Typed-yerr.Code / context.Canceled) RATHER THAN PER-SCENARIO ERROR LITERAL` — the rehearsalErrKind iota enumerates the four error shapes the reporter is documented to surface (rehearsalErrNil, rehearsalErrSentinelNoBackup, rehearsalErrTypedCodeServer, rehearsalErrContextCanceled). The scenario's wantErr field carries the kind, and assess() switches on the kind to choose the right errors.Is/errors.As predicate. Asserting on the kind (not on a literal error message) keeps the test stable across cosmetic message edits while still pinning the load-bearing typed-error contract. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint returns a documented closed-set error: don't hardcode literal messages — enumerate the kinds and switch on them in the verifier. (3) `SHARED REPORTER PER SCENARIO ACROSS BURST GOROUTINES — NOT FRESH BUILD PER GOROUTINE — TO PROVE THE DOCUMENTED CONCURRENT-SAFE CONTRACT` — backup.FileReporter is documented safe for concurrent use; building a fresh reporter per goroutine would not exercise that contract. The burst member builds one fixture per scenario UP FRONT (each fixture pairs the scenario with a built reporter), then every goroutine that draws scenario i shares the SAME reporter. A regression that introduced a shared mutable state (a memo cache, a non-snapshot read) in the reporter would surface as a goroutine seeing the WRONG scenario's predicate. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented safe for concurrent use: share the chokepoint instance across burst goroutines drawing the same scenario, never build fresh per goroutine — the burst's value is precisely the contention against the SAME instance.
ralph/progress.txt:192:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, tenant-isolation instance (BE-0398 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_tenant_isolation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/policy/tenant_isolation_test.go's new canonical pair TestTenantIsolationCoversCallSites + TestTenantIsolationPreservesScopeUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Tenant Isolation Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: tenant isolation tests (BE-0398)" subsection; TWENTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0397 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestTenantIsolation ./...`, CI step name `Tenant isolation tests`, verify.sh prefix #23 inserted between BE-0397's #22 and the trailing optionals renumbered 23->24/24->25/25->26/26->27 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0397's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 20 substrings: internal/controlplane/policy, tenant_isolation_test.go, deterministic, policy.Engine, NewEngine, ReasonDeniedCrossTenant, ReasonAllowedBySupport, ReasonAllowedSelf, ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal, ReasonDeniedPrincipalDisabled, ReasonDeniedUnknownAction, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0398-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is an authorization / policy / RBAC chokepoint in a Go package with an existing matrix-style test fixture (BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal, BE-0400 external-dokploy-smoke): (1) `SINGLE-PACKAGE INTERNAL TEST REUSING THE EXISTING TENANT-CONSTANT FIXTURES — NO NEW STORE / FAKE / RECORDER NEEDED` — the canonical pair lives in `package policy` (the SAME internal test package as the existing `policy_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `orgA` / `orgB` constants, the `principalIn` / `resourceIn` / `assertDecision` helpers, and the package-private `builtinRoleCaps` matrix verbatim. Unlike BE-0395..BE-0397 (which each introduced a new in-file concurrent fixture for the burst member because the chokepoint had observable mutable state), policy.Engine.Decide is a documented pure function with no I/O and no shared mutable state; the contention burst therefore needs no fixture at all — every goroutine constructs its own (Principal, Action, Resource) tuple from the closed-set coverage table and asserts the verdict matches the table's prediction for ITS OWN tuple. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a pure function: skip the concurrent-fixture pattern entirely; let the goroutines share the immutable Engine and assert per-iteration verdict-vs-prediction equality. (2) `THE COVERAGE TABLE AND THE BURST PREDICTION SHARE ONE SOURCE-OF-TRUTH FUNCTION` — buildTenantIsolationScenarios(t) returns a []tenantIsolationScenario where each entry pairs the input tuple with the predicted (wantAllow, wantReason). The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests; the burst member draws from the SAME table (indexed by `(w * iters + i) % len(scenarios)`) and asserts the engine's runtime verdict matches `scen.wantAllow / scen.wantReason` for the SPECIFIC tuple THIS goroutine drew. A cross-write under the race that swapped two goroutines' tuples would yield a verdict that matches the OTHER goroutine's prediction (not THIS one's), and the per-iteration assertion would fail with the offending scenario name. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set scenario table feeds both a coverage table AND a contention burst: derive both from the same builder, key the burst's per-iteration assertion on the BURST's own tuple's expectation (not the aggregate count), and surface the worker + iteration index + scenario name on every per-iteration failure so an operator can correlate the regression to a specific cross-write. (3) `ASYMMETRIC AUTHORIZE-ERROR REDACTION CONTRACT — REASON CODE IN MESSAGE ONLY FOR FORBIDDEN, NOT FOR UNAUTHENTICATED` — policy.Engine.Authorize maps ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal to apierr.Unauthenticated (HTTP 401) with a fixed `"authentication is required for action <action>"` message that does NOT echo the reason code; every other deny reason maps to apierr.Forbidden (HTTP 403) with `"not authorized for action <action> (<reason>)"`. The tenant-isolation coverage member's redaction sub-assertion therefore checks the action substring on every denial but only checks the reason substring on the Forbidden branch (skipping ReasonDeniedNoPrincipal). A regression that started echoing principal/tenant ids into either message would still surface — the per-secret loop covers both branches. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose authorization chokepoint surfaces multiple error envelopes: enumerate which fields are guaranteed in which envelope and pin the assertions per-branch, not as a single message-shape check.
ralph/progress.txt:193:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pagination-stability instance (BE-0397 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_pagination_stability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/pagination/pagination_stability_test.go's new canonical pair TestPaginationStabilityCoversCallSites + TestPaginationStabilityPreservesPagesUnderInserts + SECURITY.md "## Pagination Stability Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: pagination stability tests (BE-0397)" subsection; NINETEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0396 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestPaginationStability ./...`, CI step name `Pagination stability tests`, verify.sh prefix #22 inserted between BE-0396's #21 and the trailing optionals renumbered 22->23/23->24/24->25/25->26 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0396's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 19 substrings: internal/controlplane/pagination, pagination_stability_test.go, deterministic, fakeStore, concurrentPaginationStore, EncodeCursor, DecodeCursor, base64url, no duplicates, no drops, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0397-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a list-pagination / cursor-encoding / wire-shape invariant in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture (BE-0398 tenant isolation, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal): (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING ZERO-MUTEX SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL MUTEX-GUARDED FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package pagination` (the same internal package as the existing `page_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeStore` / `row` / `positionByID` / `rowID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file store, `concurrentPaginationStore`, with a `sync.RWMutex`-guarded rows slice and `insert` / `delete` / `list` / `listAsc` methods — declaring it in the new test file (not in `page_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeStore`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. (2) `WIDEN THE EXISTING FIXTURE WITH AN ASCENDING SIBLING METHOD WHEN THE COVERAGE SCENARIO TABLE NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS — KEEP THE DESCENDING-DEFAULT METHOD UNCHANGED` — the existing `fakeStore.list` hard-codes descending order (matches the audit-log / deployment-list canonical case). Rather than refactor it (and risk regressing the existing TestCursor* tests), add a sibling `listAsc` method on the same receiver in the new test file so the closed-set coverage member can iterate both directions through a `storeLike` interface (`list` + `listAsc`) without forking the descending fixture. The interface lives in the new test file so the existing `fakeStore` definition file is untouched. (3) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE COVERS EVERY DISTINCT PAGINATION SHAPE — EMPTY / SINGLE-ROW / EXACT-PAGE / MULTI-PAGE-NO-REMAINDER / MULTI-PAGE-PARTIAL-TRAILING / SMALL-PAGE / BOTH DIRECTIONS` — the scenario-table-rejects-duplicate-names self-check at the head of the coverage member guards against a future addition that quietly drops the closed-set property. Each scenario walks BuildPage end-to-end with a safety-bound terminator (1000-page bound) so the test fails loudly if a regression introduces a non-terminating cursor stream; the diagnostic surfaces both the scenario name AND the page index AND the row id of the divergence so an operator can correlate the gate failure with a specific in-flight traversal without re-running the suite locally. The contention burst sizing (workers=4, iterations=8, seedRows=50, pageSize=5) is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe store fails under -race ~99% of the time while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI.
